* In mainland China, Japan{{,}} and Korea, the inner dots of the character are written as {{l|mul|⿰[[⺀]]⺀}}, which is the historical form found in the Kangxi dictionary.
* In Taiwan, Hong Kong{{,}} and Vietnam, the inner dots of the character are written as {{l|mul|冫}} and its mirror image {{l|mul|⿱[[㇀]][[丶]]}}.
* When this character is used as an upper radical component, the form is different depending on the script used.
** For {{w|Ming (typefaces)|Ming typeface}} ([[明體]]/[[明朝体]]):
*** In mainland China, Japan, Korea{{,}} and Vietnam, the radical component resembles {{l|mul|⻗}} (<code>U+2ED7</code>) with four horizontal lines which is also the historical form used in the {{w|Kangxi dictionary}}.
*** In Taiwan and Hong Kong, the radical component resembles the character itself (with {{l|mul|冫}} and its mirror image) but the lower right hook of {{l|mul|冂}} is omitted.
** For {{w|regular script}} ([[楷書]]):
*** In mainland China and Japan, the radical component resembles the character itself (with {{l|mul|⿰[[⺀]]⺀}}) but the lower right hook of {{l|mul|冂}} is omitted.
*** In Taiwan and Hong Kong, the radical component resembles the character itself (with {{l|mul|冫}} and its mirror image) but the lower right hook of {{l|mul|冂}} is omitted.

